      metaDescription: "ROE vs ROIC: why the best known profitability ratio can lie, and which metric my method uses instead. Explained with real examples.",
      answer: "ROE (return on equity) measures profit against shareholders' money, but a company can push it up artificially by borrowing rather than truly improving. ROIC (return on invested capital) measures profit against ALL the capital used, debt included, making it much harder to manipulate. My method uses a cash-based variant of that second ratio, and here is why it changes everything.",
